+/**
+ * Patch 0006 (request-telemetry-jsonl): a bounded, rotated, per-request jsonl telemetry sink.
+ *
+ * Why this exists: the engine's native pino surface joins a usage block to an access line by
+ * `reqId`, but carries no session id, no model id, no TTFT, no upstream timings and no error
+ * envelope as fields — and at the only level that emits usage it also dumps up to 32 KiB of
+ * every upstream request body (system prompt + conversation) into the container log. Measured
+ * facts: `docs/tool-calling-gateway/router-telemetry-baseline.md` (inference-engine repo).
+ *
+ * Contract (kept deliberately narrow):
+ *  - Two record kinds, one JSON object per line: `progress` (in-flight, at most one per
+ *    `PROGRESS_INTERVAL_MS` per request) and `request` (terminal, written exactly once, after
+ *    that request's last `progress` line).
+ *  - Field allowlist only. Never a credential, never a header, never message content. The
+ *    record builder can only reach scalars it was explicitly handed; there is no "spread the
+ *    body/headers in" path anywhere in this file, by construction.
+ *  - Telemetry must never fail the proxy: every public entry point is wrapped so that any
+ *    throw is swallowed, and an unusable sink directory disables the sink after one warning.
+ *  - Bounded: a 64 KiB line buffer flushed on newline boundaries (whole lines only, so a
+ *    tailing reader never sees a partial line), UTC-day + size-capped rotation to
+ *    `router-YYYY-MM-DD.N.jsonl`, and at most `keepFiles` files retained.
+ *
+ * The reader — not this writer — is what marks an abandoned stream `stale` (a stream with no
+ * progress line for 120 s); the writer never emits an outcome it did not observe.
+ */
